"An earlier lesson in this unit described how a single emotion, in excess, can disturb qi in a specific, describable way — anger raising it, fear sinking it, worry knotting it. What that lesson didn't fully address is what happens when the emotional pattern isn't a single event but a habit repeated for years, and the answer this course's framework gives is that the disturbance can outlast the feeling that caused it, settling into the body as a lasting pattern rather than a passing one.": "2c4a3efe6c05",
"An earlier lesson in this unit described how a single emotion, in excess, can disturb qi in a specific, describable way — anger raising it, fear sinking it, worry knotting it. What that lesson didn't fully address is what happens when the emotional pattern isn't a single event but a habit repeated for years, and the answer this course's framework gives is that the disturbance can outlast the feeling that caused it, settling into the body as a lasting pattern rather than a passing one. A person who spent a decade in a high-conflict job, for instance, may no longer feel angry on a given day, yet still carry the physical pattern that years of frequent anger left behind — a liver qi that stays slightly too eager to move, showing up as a short temper triggered by small annoyances, or a tendency toward tension headaches that has nothing to do with that day's mood at all. The emotion that built the pattern is gone; the pattern it built remains. This is part of why this course has repeatedly separated \"an emotion\" from \"a physical pattern with that emotion's signature,\" rather than treating them as the same thing. A person can present with a liver-qi pattern while reporting they don't feel angry at all — the felt emotion and the bodily disturbance it once caused have become two different things, on two different timelines, and addressing only the mood a person reports in the moment misses the pattern that outlasted it.": "4dae7d3188b0",
